    "builtFrom": "35 documented programs",
    "body": "<p>Sort every program on this site by training days and the shape is almost comic. Fifteen run six days a week. Fourteen run five. Five run four. One runs three.</p>\n<div style=\"display:grid;gap:1px;background:#26261f;border:1px solid #26261f;margin:30px 0\"><div style=\"background:#0d0d0c;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:16px;align-items:center;padding:12px 14px\"><span style=\"font-size:15px;color:#c9c9c1\">Six days a week</span><span style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px\"><span style=\"display:block;height:8px;background:#c8ff3d;width:110px\"></span><span style=\"font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:12px;color:#c8ff3d;min-width:16px;text-align:right\">15</span></span></div><div style=\"background:#0d0d0c;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:16px;align-items:center;padding:12px 14px\"><span style=\"font-size:15px;color:#c9c9c1\">Five days</span><span style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px\"><span style=\"display:block;height:8px;background:#c8ff3d;width:103px\"></span><span style=\"font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:12px;color:#c8ff3d;min-width:16px;text-align:right\">14</span></span></div><div style=\"background:#0d0d0c;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:16px;align-items:center;padding:12px 14px\"><span style=\"font-size:15px;color:#c9c9c1\">Four days</span><span style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px\"><span style=\"display:block;height:8px;background:#c8ff3d;width:37px\"></span><span style=\"font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:12px;color:#c8ff3d;min-width:16px;text-align:right\">5</span></span></div><div style=\"background:#0d0d0c;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:16px;align-items:center;padding:12px 14px\"><span style=\"font-size:15px;color:#c9c9c1\">Three days</span><span style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px\"><span style=\"display:block;height:8px;background:#c8ff3d;width:7px\"></span><span style=\"font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:12px;color:#c8ff3d;min-width:16px;text-align:right\">1</span></span></div></div>\n<p>Which means thirty-four of the thirty-five assume a schedule that most people reading them do not have. Not because readers are lazy, but because the people in these programs were being paid to train, or training was the job itself.</p>\n<h2>What actually breaks when you cut days</h2>\n<p>The instinct is to take a six-day push/pull/legs split, run it three days a week, and accept slower progress. That isn't what happens. On a six-day PPL you hit each pattern twice a week. Run the same rotation three days a week and you hit each one every second week — chest on Monday, then not again for nine days.</p>\n<p>Frequency was doing work in that program, and you've quietly removed it while keeping all the volume per session. The result is a week that's harder to recover from and less frequent, which is the wrong trade in both directions.</p>\n<h2>The arithmetic, plainly</h2>\n<p>Take a six-day push/pull/legs rotation. Over two weeks that's four push sessions, four pull, four legs — each pattern trained roughly every three or four days. Now run the identical rotation on Monday, Wednesday and Friday. Over the same two weeks you get two of each, and the gap between two chest sessions stretches to nine days.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile the sessions themselves haven't changed. You're still doing a professional's session volume, now with less frequency and no more recovery time between the sessions you do keep. Half the training days, roughly the same weekly fatigue per session, and a third of the practice at each movement.</p>\n<h2>What to run instead</h2>\n<p>Fourteen of the thirty-five programs here are either upper/lower or full body, and those are the two structures that survive being scaled down. On three days, a full-body rotation hits everything three times a week. Upper/lower on three days alternates — two upper, one lower, then the reverse — so nothing waits more than five days.</p>\n<p>Both keep the thing that matters when time is short: how often you train a movement, not how much you pile into one session.</p>\n<h2>Two templates that survive the cut</h2>\n<p>Full body, three days: one squat or hinge, one press, one pull, one carry or single-leg movement, every session. Rotate which of those gets the heavy work each day so you're not maximal on everything three times a week. Every pattern gets trained three times weekly, which is more frequency than most six-day splits give any single pattern.</p>\n<p>Upper/lower, three days: alternate across a two-week cycle — upper, lower, upper, then lower, upper, lower. Nothing waits more than five days, and you can push session volume higher than on the full-body version because each day covers less ground.</p>\n<p>Neither is exotic. Both appear in the documented set: fourteen of the thirty-five programs are one of those two structures, and they're disproportionately the ones belonging to athletes, who cannot afford to be wrecked on a Tuesday.</p>\n<h2>What to drop first</h2>\n<p>When you cut a program down, cut isolation work before compounds, and cut the third variation of a movement before the second. If a six-day program has four separate delt exercises, you're not going to fit four into a three-day week and you don't need to — one lateral raise done twice a week outperforms four done occasionally.</p>\n<p>Keep the anchors, keep the frequency, lose the decoration. The decoration is what session count was buying.</p>\n<h2>The scaled versions are already on the site</h2>\n<p>Every one of the seven <a href=\"/splits\">full write-ups</a> carries a scaled version underneath the real week — a third of the mileage, a shorter session count, the same shape at survivable load. That section exists because printing a six-day professional block with no adaptation is how people hurt themselves and then blame the program.</p>\n<p>They're worth reading even if you have five days. The adaptation tends to reveal what the author thought was load-bearing and what was decoration.</p>\n<h2>The uncomfortable part</h2>\n<p>Three days a week done for two years beats six days a week done for five weeks, and the second one is what usually happens when somebody copies a role-prep block off a page.</p>\n<p>If you have three days, the honest answer is that you can build a genuinely good physique on them, and none of these thirty-five programs is the way to do it as written. Take the structure, take the exercise selection, ignore the calendar. <a href=\"/start\">The recommender</a> filters on days first for exactly this reason.</p>"
